{
  "teamId": 112,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Chicago Cubs arrived with championship expectations after back-to-back NL Central titles, and they largely delivered — 95 wins, a dominant August surge, and a roster full of proven talent. But the season was defined as much by its unexpected heroes as its established stars. While Javier Báez blossomed into an MVP candidate and the core of Bryant, Rizzo, and Contreras held firm, it was rookie David Bote who etched his name into Cubs lore with a series of clutch home runs that became the emotional centerpiece of a memorable summer.\n\nThe early months brought the usual turbulence of a long season. The bullpen leaked in March and the team played inconsistent baseball through May, with costly late-inning collapses undercutting what the offense was building. But even in those uneven stretches, Báez was becoming a force — his sharp double in April and a go-ahead homer in May showed he was ready to carry moments. June brought more bullpen heartbreak, including a grand slam surrendered by Justin Wilson and a rough outing from Randy Rosario, yet the Cubs still finished 16-12 for the month, a sign of real underlying strength.\n\nThe season's emotional peak arrived in August, when Bote's walk-off grand slam against the Nationals on August 12th — one of the highest-leverage plays of the entire year — sent Wrigley into delirium and became the defining image of the Cubs' 19-10 month. That historic blast capped a summer run that saw Chicago pull away in the division, propelled by timely hitting up and down the lineup, strong starting pitching, and a defense anchored by Báez's brilliance. The team looked ready for a deep postseason run.\n\nInstead, October delivered a gut-punch. Facing the Colorado Rockies in the NL Wild Card game — a result of a late-season stumble that cost them the division title — the Cubs were unable to manufacture runs when it mattered most. Anthony Rizzo grounded into a double play, Jason Heyward struck out swinging, and Kyle Hendricks surrendered a crucial single to Tony Wolters that broke the game open. Even a Báez double couldn't ignite a comeback. A 95-win season that had promised so much ended in a single night at Wrigley, another chapter in the complicated, brilliant, and still-unfinished story of this Cubs core.",
